{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788975785,"nonce":"S8kc/EcMGUr1uYWc3sOQJgwkWz0=","title":"ASH_GC_STRESS runs out of memory on binary_trees","message":"`bench_binary_trees` (and its lighter n=14 variant) aborts under\n`ASH_GC_STRESS=1` with \"Out of memory\" from `hlp_alloc_obj`\n(`std/src/obj.rs:134`), while completing checksum-correct in every engine\nwithout stress mode.\n\nThe workload is millions of short-lived 3-field objects around one long-lived\ntree -- the worst case for block-level reclamation with conservative scanning,\nsince a block survives if ANY stale stack word points into it, and this maximizes\nboth block count and plausible-pointer integers on the stack.\n\nSuspects in order: false retention pinning nearly every block until the heap\nfills; stress mode collecting so often the proactive trigger never sees a\ngenuinely empty block; a leak in the stress path itself.\n\nIt means the GC stress gate currently proves less than it claims.","files":null}]}